[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Yeah, but how long do the batteries in an electric car last? What do they do when they get out of warranty? Don\'t you have to change them every 100,000 mi? Those are sort of comments I still see regularly on internet comments on our videos. And just when I start speaking to people, you know, the uncertainty'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about how long the battery will last in an electric car. Oh, I\'m not sure about that. I\'ll keep running my diesel, thank you very much. Um so, what I\'ve done is gather over 600 results from battery'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tests on used electric cars. I\'ve tried to gather as many as I can from high mileage cars and over 100,000 mi. I\'ve got two cars we run in the company here, a Tesla Model 3 and Model Y. And'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'re on 247,000 mi and 210,000 mi. All original and running great. So, I\'ll cover a bit of information about them in this video as well. But basically, what I want to do in this video is try to'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'answer some of those sort of skeptic questions and show you the data I\'ve got. Now, I\'ve tried to sort of summarize it and I\'ll do a couple of graphs but try and keep it as brief as I can to show you just how well EV'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'batteries last as you get more and more miles in your car, over 100,000 mi, over 200,000 mi. Typically with no maintenance as well, by the way, or very little maintenance. No clutches, no cam'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'belts, no exhaust, no water pumps, all that kind of stuff. Forget all that. Typically very low maintenance. What are they like over 100,000 mi? And I mentioned 100,000 mi cuz it\'s typically what a lot of people consider a higher'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mileage car. Not really these days, not for petrol, diesel, or electric, but some people do. And more relevant, it\'s often the point at which a manufacturer\'s warranty will expire on a battery in an electric car. So, you'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'know, what\'s the risk afterwards? Um well, there are warranties available and I can mention some of that in this video. Uh but let\'s try and crack into it. It\'s going to be me talking a lot and I\'ll try and show a couple of graphs, but um'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stick with me. I\'ll try and cover all bases and include in I want to share the information I\'ve got so far on how an LFP battery compared to a non-LFP battery cuz they always talk about them lasting longer. So, I\'ll share what I\'ve got there as well.'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Right, straight into first graph. Here is a summary of kind of everything so far in one graph. As simplistic as I can put it, but what I will point out is there are variations in how you can interpret'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'battery test results. So, the cars can report them slightly differently. Different software will analyze the battery slightly differently. There are number of variables, but we try to keep as many consistencies as we can here'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using the same battery test method. Typically we do an average test on this actually and sometimes we we verify by plugging in the testers and testing ourselves with scan my tester. So, and it tends to be a very consistent result,'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but there are other battery tests and they\'re out there which will give slightly different results. And cars like Hyundai and Kia tend to report very high levels of battery state of health compared to other cars. It\'s probably'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really more about how the car reports it and what we\'ve got for buffers. So, a bit of pinch of salt, but there\'s over 600 results in this graph you\'re looking at now and and therefore we\'ve got the kind of line of best fit. And what we\'re showing here is the degradation of a'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'battery. There is no they just drop off a cliff. They just stop working. They\'re just no good anymore. They just degrade and the usable capacity'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will gradually reduce over time. So, what we\'re showing here is SOH, state of health. How much of that battery is still available to be used by that vehicle today. So, the um'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Y axis is that state of health and the X axis is the mileage. And again, to just summarize stuff a little bit here, we can see that the crucial 100,000 mi'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'point there batteries in electric cars across all different manufacturers typically average out at about 90% of their original capacity. And what you can see from that graph, the line of kind of'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'best fit there, is that you will get some initial degradation, which actually is is higher the first 30,000 mi. You you might well lose a few percent, but then the level of degradation slows down over time and actually'

